## std::array — Array (Ruby's Array) method surface.
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 2 (Option C). `class Array[T]`
## below carries BOTH the FFI alias decls (`Array_<m>` →
## `ruxen_vec_<m>`, consumed by `Lowerer::resolve_ffi_alias_callee`)
## AND the TRUE surface return types that typeck delegates to via the
## builtin-receiver bridge (`InferenceEngine::bridge_builtin_method`,
## reached for `Ty::Array(_)` because `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head`
## now includes the Array head). `method_home_key` maps `Ty::Array(_)` →
## `"Array"` (generic suffix stripped); element substitution into the
## declared `T` is done by `substitute_generics_in_return`.
##
## `Array` is NOT in `register_builtins`'s type-scope map — `let x:
## Array[Int]` resolves through the hardcoded arm in `resolve_type_expr`
## that builds `Ty::Array(Int)` directly. The anchor branch creates a
## `DefId` for FFI bookkeeping without inserting into type-scope, so the
## hardcoded type resolver stays authoritative for the receiver TYPE
## while the class below homes the METHODS.
##
## ## Generic-stripping at the call site
##
## MIR mangles the surface generic in the call: `Vec[Int]_push`,
## `Array[String]_clone`, etc. The class shell registers under the
## generic-free parent name `Array`, so the alias-map key is
## `Array_push` / `Array_clone`. The lookup site in
## `mir/lower/expr/method_call.rs` peels the `[...]` segment from the
## mangled callee and retries with the generic-stripped key.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## Every migrated method's true surface type is ABI-identical to its C
## symbol's wire width: `RuxenVec*` / `void*` / boxed `Option` cross the
## C ABI as I64, `uint64_t` (len/count/capacity) as I64 (= `USize`), and
## `T`-by-value `remove` is I64 for every element head. `ty_to_cranelift`
## lowers each to the matching width; the parity guard
## `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins that the derived widths equal
## the hand-rolled `runtime_signature` table.
##
## ## Iteration — `each` is a real `.rx` body, not a laundered decl
##
##   * `each(f: Fn(T) -> nil)`: called with a closure value
##     (`arr.each(f)` / `arr.each(&block)`) it runs the `.rx` body below;
##     a literal trailing block is MIR-inlined (closure_inline) and never
##     reaches it. `select!` has no decl: its literal block is MIR-inlined
##     the same way and the collections.rs arm types it. The other closure
##     combinators are `Enumerable` defaults over `each`.
##
## ## Residual — typed by the collections.rs resolver arms that run
## ## AHEAD of the bridge (these decls below stay laundered to `Int`
## ## purely for the FFI alias / codegen call rewrite; the resolver arm
## ## supplies the user-visible type)
##
##   * `zip` / `to_h`: the result element type is computed from the ARG
##     (`zip`) or the receiver's tuple element (`to_h`) — not expressible
##     as a static declared return — so the resolver arm computes it.
##     Their decls stay for the FFI alias (`ruxen_vec_zip` /
##     `ruxen_hash_from_iter` emit real calls).
##   * `get_mut` / `get_var`: alias `ruxen_vec_get_opt` with a `&var T`
##     return that differs from `get`'s `&T`; a second `.rx` alias for
##     the same C symbol with a different wire shape trips E0722. Kept as
##     a lang_intrinsics alias + collections.rs resolver arm.
class Array[T]
  lib "runtime/vec.c"
    # ── migrated: true surface types, delegated via the bridge ──
    def self.new as "ruxen_vec_new" -> Array[T]
    def self.with_capacity as "ruxen_vec_with_capacity"(cap: Int) -> Array[T]
    def size as "ruxen_vec_len" -> USize
    def capacity as "ruxen_vec_capacity" -> USize
    def count as "ruxen_vec_count" -> USize
    def empty? as "ruxen_vec_is_empty" -> Bool
    def get as "ruxen_vec_get_opt"(i: Int) -> Option[&T]
    # `get_mut` / `get_var` alias the SAME C symbol as `get`
    # (`ruxen_vec_get_opt`); they differ only in surface mutability of
    # the borrowed element (`&var T` vs `get`'s `&T`). Both lower to a
    # pointer and the boxed `Option` is one pointer, so all three share
    # the WIRE shape `(self:I64, i:I64) -> Option:I64`. The E0722 check
    # (relaxed to compare post-self-prepend wire widths,
    # `resolve/ffi_registration.rs`) admits these second/third aliases.
    def get_mut as "ruxen_vec_get_opt"(i: Int) -> Option[&var T]
    def get_var as "ruxen_vec_get_opt"(i: Int) -> Option[&var T]
    # `first` / `last` hand back the element itself (the C side does not
    # copy it), so the payload is a borrow, like `get`'s: `.clone` it to keep.
    def first as "ruxen_vec_first" -> Option[&T]
    def last as "ruxen_vec_last" -> Option[&T]
    def clone as "ruxen_vec_clone" -> Array[T]
    def reverse as "ruxen_vec_reverse" -> Array[T]
    def push as "ruxen_vec_push"(x: T)
    def pop as "ruxen_vec_pop" -> Option[T]
    def clear as "ruxen_vec_clear"
    def truncate as "ruxen_vec_truncate"(n: Int)
    def swap as "ruxen_vec_swap"(i: Int, j: Int)
    def insert as "ruxen_vec_insert"(i: Int, x: T)
    def remove as "ruxen_vec_remove"(i: Int) -> T
    def extend as "ruxen_vec_extend"(other: &Array[T])
    # Ruby `take(n)` / `drop(n)` eager-materialise a fresh Array.
    def take as "ruxen_vec_take"(n: Int) -> Array[T]
    def drop as "ruxen_vec_skip"(n: Int, elems: ElemDesc[T]) -> Array[T]
    # `a.chain(b)` concatenates two arrays directly.
    def chain as "ruxen_vec_chain"(other: &Array[T], elems: ElemDesc[T]) -> Array[T]
    # `arr.to_set` → Set[T].
    def to_set as "ruxen_set_from_iter"(keys: KeyDesc[T]) -> Set[T]
    # ── residual: laundered `Int` decl kept ONLY for the FFI alias;
    #    the collections.rs resolver (ahead of the bridge) supplies the
    #    user-visible return type. See header.
    #    The pairs' descriptor lets C copy each pair the way its drop frees it;
    #    the table's key descriptor is bound from the result.
    def to_h[K, V] as "ruxen_hash_from_iter"(pairs: ElemDesc[T], keys: KeyDesc[K]) -> Hash[K, V]
  end

  # Ruby `each` with a closure VALUE: `arr.each(f)` / `arr.each(&block)`.
  # A literal trailing block (`arr.each { |x| ... }`) never reaches this
  # body: MIR inlines it (closure_inline). The param is `Fn(T) -> nil`, so
  # a trailing block bound here settles as a borrowing Block.
  def each(f: Fn(T) -> nil) -> nil
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      f.(self[i])
      i = i + 1
    end
    nil
  end

  # ── Enumerable combinators ─────────────────────────────────────────
  # `map` / `select` / `reject` / `all?` / `any?` / `partition` /
  # `each_with_index` / `find` / `index` / `reduce` are defined ONCE in
  # `mixin Enumerable[T]` (core/lib.rx) over the `each` primitive and
  # pulled in here by the include below — no per-class bodies. Typeck
  # resolves them through the builtin-receiver bridge: `Ty::Array(_)`'s
  # `method_home_key` is `"Array"`, the include registers the
  # `("Array", "Enumerable")` nominal impl, and the bridge's
  # trait-default-method arm supplies each signature (the trailing block
  # is the closure arg; method-level `[U]` is harvested from the
  # closure's return). MIR emits ONE opaque `Array_<m>` body per
  # combinator (type params abstract — sound because the bodies only
  # push / closure-call / iterate, never dispatch on `T`); the call site
  # routes the generic-suffixed `Array[Int]_map` callee to it via
  # `lib_body_methods` + `strip_generic_suffix`. Every combinator is
  # expressed over `each`: a literal block passed to `each` is MIR-inlined
  # (closure_inline/each.rs), and a closure value calls the `.rx` `each`
  # body below.
  #
  # A mixin default that DID need to dispatch on `T` would NOT be covered
  # by the per-`T` monomorphization below: that pass specializes a class's
  # OWN body methods, while an included default is emitted through
  # `lower_impl_block_with_outer_methods`. Extending it to included
  # defaults is what `Enumerable#sum` / `min_by` still wait on.
  include Enumerable[T]

  # Ruby `sort_by` — in-place comparator sort. The closure returns a
  # negative / zero / positive `Int` ordering of its two arguments; a
  # positive result means the pair is out of order, so they are
  # swapped. Selection-sort shape over the migrated `swap` primitive
  # and indexed reads (`self[i]`); mutates the receiver, yields `nil`.
  def sort_by(cmp: Fn(T, T) -> Int) -> nil
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var j: Int = i + 1
      while j < n
        if cmp.(self[i], self[j]) > 0
          self.swap(i, j)
        end
        j = j + 1
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    nil
  end

  # Ruby `zip` — pair each element with the corresponding element of
  # `other`, truncating to the shorter length. Expressed over indexed
  # reads (`self[i]` / `other[i]`); the result element type `(T, U)` is
  # a static declared return, so no resolver arm is needed. (The
  # `ruxen_vec_zip` C symbol is retired — the `.rx` body materialises
  # the pairs directly.)
  def zip[U](other: Array[U]) -> Array[(T, U)]
    var out: Array[(T, U)] = Array.new
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var m: Int = other.size as Int
    var limit: Int = n
    if m < n
      limit = m
    end
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < limit
      out.push((self[i], other[i]))
      i = i + 1
    end
    out
  end

  # ── per-`T` monomorphized combinators ──────────────────────────────
  # These bodies DISPATCH on the element type — they interpolate an
  # element, order two elements, or compare two elements — so MIR
  # specializes one copy per concrete element type actually called
  # (`Array__mono__String_join`). See `mir/lower/monomorphize.rs`
  # "per-`T` monomorphization for BUILTIN-HEAD generic classes".
  #
  # The element must be read into a `var x: T` binding first: `self[i]`
  # itself types as an inference variable inside the shell body, and only
  # the annotated `let` carries the `T` that substitution rewrites to the
  # concrete element type.

  # Ruby `join` — concatenate every element's display form, separated by
  # `sep`. `[].join == ""`, and the separator defaults to `", "`.
  def join(sep: &String = ", ") -> String
    var out: String = ""
    var i: Int = 0
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    while i < n
      if i > 0
        out = out + "#{sep}"
      end
      var x: T = self[i]
      out = out + "#{x}"
      i = i + 1
    end
    out
  end

  # Ruby `min` / `max` — the smallest / largest element by the element
  # type's own ordering, or `nil` for an empty array.
  def min -> Option[T]
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    if n == 0
      return nil
    end
    var best: T = self[0]
    var i: Int = 1
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      if x < best
        best = x
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    Option.Some(best)
  end

  def max -> Option[T]
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    if n == 0
      return nil
    end
    var best: T = self[0]
    var i: Int = 1
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      if x > best
        best = x
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    Option.Some(best)
  end

  # Ruby `uniq` — a fresh array keeping the FIRST occurrence of each
  # distinct element, in original order. Membership is decided by the
  # element type's own `==` (the specialization is what makes that
  # compare contents rather than raw words).
  def uniq -> Array[T]
    var out: Array[T] = Array.new
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      var seen: Bool = false
      var j: Int = 0
      var m: Int = out.size as Int
      while j < m
        var y: T = out[j]
        if x == y
          seen = true
        end
        j = j + 1
      end
      if seen == false
        out.push(x)
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    out
  end

  # Ruby `include?` — true when some element is `==` to `x`, by the
  # element type's own equality (String by content, Float by value).
  def include?(x: T) -> Bool
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var y: T = self[i]
      if y == x
        return true
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    false
  end

  # Ruby `==` — same length and pairwise `==` elements, by the element
  # type's own equality.
  def ==(other: Array[T]) -> Bool
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    if n != other.size as Int
      return false
    end
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      var y: T = other[i]
      if x != y
        return false
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # Ruby `sort` — a NEW array of the elements in ascending order by the
  # element type's own `<` (stable); the receiver is left unchanged.
  def sort -> Array[T]
    var out: Array[T] = Array.new
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      out.push(x)
      i = i + 1
    end
    out.sort!
    out
  end

  # Ruby `sort!` — sort the receiver in place, ascending by the element
  # type's own `<` (insertion sort over `swap`, so it is stable).
  def sort! -> nil
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 1
    while i < n
      var j: Int = i
      while j > 0
        var a: T = self[j - 1]
        var b: T = self[j]
        if b < a
          self.swap(j - 1, j)
          j = j - 1
        else
          j = 0
        end
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    nil
  end

  # Rust-style `dedup` — remove consecutive `==` duplicates in place,
  # keeping the first of each run.
  def dedup -> nil
    var i: Int = 1
    while i < self.size as Int
      var a: T = self[i - 1]
      var b: T = self[i]
      if a == b
        self.remove(i)
      else
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # Ruby `sum` — the elements added with the element type's own `+`,
  # starting from zero (`[].sum == 0`).
  def sum -> T where T: Add
    var total: T = 0
    var n: Int = self.size as Int
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < n
      var x: T = self[i]
      total = total + x
      i = i + 1
    end
    total
  end

  # Ruby `to_a` is a synonym of `clone` (both shallow-copy via
  # `ruxen_vec_clone`). Declared as a pure `alias` instead of a second
  # FFI decl — both names resolve to the ONE `Array_clone` body, no
  # duplicated codegen (docs/decisions/alias-keyword.md).
  alias to_a clone

  # Ruby `Array#+` — a fresh array with both operands' elements in order
  # (`[1, 2] + [3, 4] == [1, 2, 3, 4]`). A `.rx` operator method delegating to
  # `chain`; the binop lowering routes `a + b` here (builtin-head operator
  # route) instead of the machine floor, which would integer-add the two heap
  # pointers and miscompile.
  def +(other: Array[T]) -> Array[T]
    self.chain(other)
  end
end
